// `@prelude` says something about a *file*, so it is written `@!prelude` and
// belongs to no item. Without the `!` it is an ordinary item attribute, and an
// ordinary item attribute at file scope attaches to whatever declaration comes
// next - here `main` would silently absorb a mark meant for the module, and the
// program would compile with no prelude having been claimed at all.
//@ error: it applies to a module, and is written `@!prelude`

@prelude
@export
proc main() i32 {
    return 0;
}
